From 0963257225b6d65ddef6fd43178951c3f14dc383 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adam Szewczyk <a.szewczyk@cthings.co>
Date: Fri, 3 Jul 2026 13:50:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] boards: ct: ctcc: lock APPROTECT on production firmware

The in-repo ctcc boards set CONFIG_NRF_APPROTECT_LOCK=y, so first boot of a
secure image locked the UICR APPROTECT debug-access-port protection. Moving the
board to the upstream NCS Zephyr fork dropped that default, so an image built for
this board no longer locks the debug port unless it asks for that itself.

Restore it at board level through the NRF_APPROTECT_HANDLING choice default in
Kconfig.defconfig, so it applies to every ctcc image - application and MCUboot,
all SoC variants - and default the nRF91-only NRF_SECURE_APPROTECT_HANDLING
choice to LOCK as well.

The two SoCs then lock by different mechanisms, each the one its part supports:

  nRF52840 has a single access port, protected by a UICR word that nothing in
  Zephyr or NCS ever writes - SystemInit only loads the soft branch from it. The
  app_protect SYS_INIT helper (drivers/misc/app_protect, CTCC_APP_PROTECT=y)
  performs that write and resets on first boot of a secure image, so the lock is
  permanent and re-flashing over SWD needs `nrfjprog --recover` first.

  nRF91 has two access ports, and NCS handles both: with these choices at LOCK,
  SystemInit sets APPROTECT.FORCEPROTECT and SECUREAPPROTECT.FORCEPROTECT on
  every boot. Left at the upstream default the secure port is not merely skipped
  but actively unprotected - the MDK writes SECUREAPPROTECT.DISABLE when
  UICR.SECUREAPPROTECT reads HwUnprotected - so a card locked on the non-secure
  port alone stays fully readable over SWD through the secure one.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5[1m]
Signed-off-by: Adam Szewczyk <a.szewczyk@cthings.co>
---
 boards/ct/ctcc/Kconfig.defconfig | 22 ++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+)

diff --git a/boards/ct/ctcc/Kconfig.defconfig b/boards/ct/ctcc/Kconfig.defconfig
index c8ebd846a1e..e9ad80dadb9 100644
--- a/boards/ct/ctcc/Kconfig.defconfig
+++ b/boards/ct/ctcc/Kconfig.defconfig
@@ -9,5 +9,27 @@ source "boards/common/usb/Kconfig.cdc_acm_serial.defconfig"
 
 endif # BOARD_CTCC_NRF52840
 
+# Lock APPROTECT (debug read-back protection) on production ctcc firmware. The
+# app_protect SYS_INIT helper (drivers/misc/app_protect, CTCC_APP_PROTECT=y)
+# writes UICR.APPROTECT and resets on first boot of a secure image; selecting
+# the LOCK choice member here engages it for every ctcc image (app + MCUboot).
+choice NRF_APPROTECT_HANDLING
+	default NRF_APPROTECT_LOCK
+endchoice
+
+# nRF91 has a SECOND access port with its own UICR word, and its choice is left at
+# NRF_SECURE_APPROTECT_USE_UICR upstream - which does not merely skip the secure AP,
+# it actively unprotects it (the MDK writes SECUREAPPROTECT.DISABLE = SwUnprotected
+# when UICR.SECUREAPPROTECT reads HwUnprotected, see system_nrf91_approtect.h). A
+# card locked only on the non-secure AP is still fully readable over SWD through the
+# secure one. Default this choice to LOCK as well so SystemInit force-protects both.
+# The choice depends on the nRF91 series, so on nRF52840 this block is present but
+# INVISIBLE - the Kconfig text is unconditional and at file top level; what the
+# dependency removes is the choice's visibility, so the default simply never applies - which has a single access port and gets its lock from the UICR write
+# performed by drivers/misc/app_protect.
+choice NRF_SECURE_APPROTECT_HANDLING
+	default NRF_SECURE_APPROTECT_LOCK
+endchoice
+
 config BOARD_CTCC
 	select USE_DT_CODE_PARTITION if BOARD_CTCC_NRF9161_NS || BOARD_CTCC_NRF9151_NS
-- 
2.43.0

